# ── DOUBLY LINKED LIST ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Two independent chains of pointers over the same nodes, drawn as two rows of arrows so
# the reader can count them. The cost a doubly linked list charges is exactly this: every
# structural change must fix BOTH chains, and the second one is the one people forget.
#
# Only the tail terminator is drawn. A left-hand null is a back-edge from the first node,
# and ELK parks it below the row where it reads as part of the tangle rather than as an
# end — say "head.prev is null too" in prose instead of fighting the layout for it.
#
# Knobs: node count; the terminator.

...@../lib/theme

direction: right

n0: 3 { class: node }
n1: 7 { class: node }
n2: 1 { class: node }
nil_r: "∅" { class: [node; cold] }

n0 -> n1: next
n1 -> n2: next
n2 -> nil_r: next

n1 -> n0: prev { class: hint }
n2 -> n1: prev { class: hint }
